# NestJS Code Review

Review NestJS backend changes across five dimensions. Only report findings that apply; skip empty sections. For each finding: file/line, issue, why it matters, concrete fix. End with Critical / Should Fix / Nice to Have.

## 1. Code Standards

- No unnecessary `any`; type DTOs, entities, and service return types.
- DTOs use `class-validator` / `class-transformer` on every input field.
- Controllers stay thin; business logic in services; Nest exceptions only (`BadRequestException`, etc.).
- Config via `ConfigService`, not scattered `process.env`.
- **Enums:** repeated domain strings (roles, statuses, error codes) use feature `enums/*.enum.ts` string enums — no magic strings in services/guards/tests. DTOs use `@IsEnum(...)`.

## 2. Reusable Components

- Shared pagination, validators, and response helpers live in `common/` or shared modules — do not copy across features.
- Prefer `@CurrentUser()`, role guards, pipes, and interceptors over duplicated `req.user` / inline checks.
- Reuse common DTOs; avoid barrel `index.ts` files that create circular imports.

## 3. Security

- Global `ValidationPipe` with `whitelist` + `forbidNonWhitelisted`.
- Auth via Passport/JWT guards; authorize every protected route server-side.
- **Tenant isolation:** org-scoped data must use `organizationId` from the JWT/user context — never trust client-supplied org IDs alone. See `docs/architecture/access-control.md`.
- Role checks (`USER` / `ADMIN` / `SUPERADMIN`) enforced with shared enums + guards, not string compares.
- Never log or return secrets/tokens/passwords; hash passwords; parameterize all ORM queries.
- Throttle auth and abuse-prone routes; validate upload type/size; no hardcoded secrets.

## 4. Performance

- Avoid N+1; use joins/`relations` deliberately; paginate list endpoints.
- Index columns used in frequent filters/sorts; cache hot reads when justified.
- Offload heavy work to BullMQ; no unbounded `find()` result sets.

## 5. Architecture

- Feature modules, constructor DI only, no circular module imports.
- Cross-feature access via exported providers, not deep imports into another feature’s internals.
- Unit tests for services; align API field names with `client/src/types/`.
